<h2 id="quote">The Quote</h2>
<p>In a wide-ranging profile published by The Athletic this week, Johnson laid out goals well beyond the standard training-camp coach-speak. "You've got 31 other teams coming to training camp saying, 'Our goal is to win the Super Bowl,'" Johnson told The Athletic's Dan Pompei. "I want more than that. I want to have the highest-scoring offense ever, which happens to be the 2013 Denver Broncos. I know how many points per game they scored, and I want to surpass them. I want shutouts. I want blowouts." He's also chasing the NFL's consecutive-victories record of 21, set by Bill Belichick's early-2000s Patriots dynasty.</p>
<h2 id="record">The Record Johnson Is Chasing</h2>
<p>The 2013 Broncos, led by Peyton Manning in the best statistical season of his career, scored 37.9 points per game — the highest full-season scoring average in NFL history. That Denver offense set the still-standing records for points (606) and touchdowns (76) in a season. Every team that has scored 35-plus points per game in a season, including that Broncos squad, has failed to win the Super Bowl that same year, a historical wrinkle that makes Johnson's dual goals of a scoring record and a title an even steeper combined climb.</p>
