<h2 id="personnel">The Personnel Case</h2>
<p>Johnson's confidence isn't coming from nowhere. He's the same play-caller who helped turn Detroit's offense into a perennial top-five unit and made Amon-Ra St. Brown one of the league's most productive receivers. Chicago's decision to trade away veteran DJ Moore this offseason is being read internally as a vote of confidence in younger receivers Rome Odunze and Luther Burden III, both of whom Johnson believes have more room to grow than a proven veteran does. Caleb Williams, entering his third season, is exactly the kind of mobile, improvisational quarterback that tends to unlock extra scoring: the same profile that's shown up across other recent high-speed rookie and sophomore quarterbacks league-wide.</p>
