<h2 id="reality">The Case Against It</h2>
<p>The math is the biggest obstacle. Going from 25.9 to 37.9 points per game isn't an incremental jump — it's the difference between a good offense and the single best scoring season in NFL history, achieved by a future Hall of Fame quarterback at the peak of his powers behind one of the more talented supporting casts ever assembled. Chicago also faces the league's toughest schedule in 2026, a first-place slate loaded with divisional games against the Lions plus matchups against the Eagles, Patriots, Seahawks, and Bills — a sharp contrast to the relatively soft schedule Denver played in 2013. A defense that was often the weak link in 2025 adds another variable Johnson doesn't fully control.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is the NFL record for points scored in a season?</div>
<div class="faq-a">The 2013 Denver Broncos hold the record with 606 points in a 16-game season, an average of 37.9 points per game, led by Peyton Manning in his highest-scoring statistical season.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Has any team that averaged 35+ points per game ever won the Super Bowl that season?</div>
<div class="faq-a">No. Every team in NFL history to average 35 or more points per game in a season, including the record-setting 2013 Broncos, has fallen short of winning the Super Bowl that same year.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How much would the Bears need to improve to break the scoring record?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Chicago averaged 25.9 points per game in 2025. Breaking Denver's 37.9 mark would require an increase of roughly 12 points per game, a jump from ninth in the league to first all-time.</div>
</div>
